From: Garnet local integration
Subject: [PATCH 32/32] aco/spill: bound spill_ctx memory with IDSet and dead-block spill-map release

Large compute shaders with heavy register spilling (such as Blender Cycles
MetalRT uber-shaders translated to SPIR-V, ~99k ACO blocks and ~157k spill IDs
with ~240M interference edges) caused aco::spill phys_footprint to explode past
12-29 GiB on 16 GiB macOS hosts due to two unbounded allocations in spill_ctx:

1. spill_ctx::interferences stored std::unordered_set<uint32_t> per spill ID,
   allocating two libc++ hash-table nodes plus bucket storage (~96 B/edge) via
   operator new. Replacing it with aco::IDSet backed by spill_ctx::memory (held
   in a std::deque for stable addresses with O(1) indexing) cuts 240M edges
   from ~23 GiB down to ~301 MiB used.
2. spills_entry and spills_exit used aco::unordered_map backed by the
   non-reclaiming monotonic_buffer_resource across all ~99k blocks, and
   spill_block copied spills_entry -> spills_exit without reserve(), leaking
   rehash buckets and retaining ~9.5 GiB used / 16 GiB slab of dead per-block
   spill maps. Moving spills_entry/spills_exit (and loop_info::spills) to
   std::unordered_map, moving spills_entry -> spills_exit for non-loop-header
   blocks, saving only top-level SGPR spill IDs for end_unused_spill_vgprs(),
   and releasing spills_exit[pred] once all predecessor-reading successors
   (and loop-header coupling code) finish keeps only the active CFG frontier
   in memory.

Together these cut cycles_metal_integrator_shade_shadow from >29.9 GiB Jetsam
OOM down to 2,156 MiB total peak (1,638 MiB during aco::spill) in 230.9s with
identical spill slot assignments.

Base: f5cb8ee032adabef599ae892ec4e42d796b84da9 + patches 0001..0031.

